    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Musok is a polytheistic religion without doctrine or central authority, in which ritual specialists called mudang, most of them women, mediate between people and the gods and ancestral spirits.</li><li>The mudang's central ritual is the gut, a performance of rhythmic movement, song, oracles and offerings in which the spirits are entertained and, through the mudang, speak directly to the living.</li><li>Suppressed under the Joseon dynasty, by modernisers, by the Japanese and by South Korean governments, Musok has since the late 1970s been increasingly seen as a source of Korean cultural identity, though the mudang remain widely stigmatised.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Mudang say that the gods choose them: a person who resists the calling may be tormented with misfortune, illness or madness, called sinbyŏng, until they accept it and are trained by an established mudang.</p></aside>
    </section>

