    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">12th century</span><span class="rf-text">The term mu is first recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, in the Yisanggugjip and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a>, which describe spirit mediumship, exorcism, divination and ancestor veneration</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">13th century</span><span class="rf-text">Images of Musok deities are first recorded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1398</span><span class="rf-text">In an extreme case of the persecution of mudang under the Joseon dynasty, a mudang is beheaded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1702</span><span class="rf-text">Jeju governor Yi Hyŏngsang purges the island's simbang and destroys 129 shrines</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1896</span><span class="rf-text">Police launch a crackdown, arresting mudang, destroying shrines and burning paraphernalia</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">The Japanese colonial government launches the Mind Cultivation Movement, among its measures to suppress Musok</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1970s</span><span class="rf-text">The Saemaul Undong, or New Community Movement, brings a surge in police suppression of mudang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, and they form a federation to promote their interests</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2009</span><span class="rf-text">The Yŏngdŭng gut performed at Ch'ilmŏri Shrine on Jeju is added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity</span></li></ol>
